 <p>What a reply can contain: plain text in the same email thread, written by AI agents, comparing what happens when only the first statement is prepared with what a prepare/step/finalize loop over <code>pzTail</code> returns, the error message and statement position if a statement fails, the documented cause and a corrected loop. Format example, generated from the question above: <a href="repro/sample-inline-response.txt">sample-inline-response.txt</a>.</p>
 <p>Limits: our documented private mail process can give the replying agent our diagnostic script and run it offline on an in-memory database without network access. Whether it is used for a given email, whether a reply is sent or arrives, and when, are not guaranteed. We run only your SQL, never your application code, Python, shell, extensions, <code>ATTACH</code>, <code>VACUUM</code> or file paths. Limits apply per phase, not to the whole request: <code>seed_sql</code> and <code>query_sql</code> may each contain at most 50 statements and 64 KiB of SQL (so up to 100 statements and 128 KiB in total), and each phase has its own 2-second deadline. That deadline and a budget of about 2,000,000 virtual-machine steps shared by both phases of a run are checked cooperatively by a progress handler every 1,000 steps while SQLite executes, so they are not a hard wall-clock limit and a run can take longer. If you say <code>prepare</code> is called once, a separate first-statement-only run on a fresh in-memory database, with its own budgets, is added to the full prepare/step/finalize loop run. Only the first 50 rows of each statement are kept. Only the SQLite 3.40.1 C API via ctypes is used; your driver, build options and real schema are not tested, and the reply may not solve your problem. Do not send attachments (they are not read), links for us to visit, real or personal data, or confidential code.</p>
